LIME — Theory
The explanation for instance \(\mathbf{x}\) is the model \(g\) from a family \(G\) of interpretable models that minimises:
\[
\text{explanation}(\mathbf{x}) \;=\; \arg\min_{g \in G}\;
\underbrace{L\!\left(\widehat{f},\, g,\, \pi_{\mathbf{x}}\right)}_{\text{local fidelity}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{\Omega(g)}_{\text{simplicity}}
\]

\(\widehat{f}\) — the black-box model;
\(\pi_{\mathbf{x}}\) — a proximity kernel around \(\mathbf{x}\) (typically exponential);
\(L\) — weighted loss (e.g. weighted MSE);
\(\Omega(g)\) — complexity penalty (e.g. number of non-zero coefficients).

The LIME algorithm:

Pick the instance \(\mathbf{x}\) to explain.
Perturb it to create a synthetic neighbourhood and predict with \(\widehat{f}\).
Weight each perturbation by its proximity to \(\mathbf{x}\).
Fit a sparse linear model on the weighted sample.
Read off the coefficients — those are the explanation.

Decoder Block in TensorFlow 2

class DecoderBlock(tf.keras.layers.Layer):
    def __init__(self, d_model, num_heads):
        super().__init__()
        self.attn = tf.keras.layers.MultiHeadAttention(num_heads, d_model)
        self.ffn = tf.keras.Sequential([
            tf.keras.layers.Dense(4*d_model, activation='relu'),
            tf.keras.layers.Dense(d_model)
        ])
        self.norm1 = tf.keras.layers.LayerNormalization()
        self.norm2 = tf.keras.layers.LayerNormalization()

    def call(self, x, mask=None):
        attn_output = self.attn(x, x, attention_mask=mask)
        x = self.norm1(x + attn_output)
        ffn_output = self.ffn(x)
        return self.norm2(x + ffn_output)
